# Installing: a person with an empty library adds a folder, and the real
# owner compiles it and its dependencies (offline, from the local cargo
# cache). Expected words come from the project's own files: its folder name,
# its Cargo.toml, its Cargo.lock.

# first-run: the empty Library, as a first launch shows it.
part first-run
check first-run
  line "0 projects · 0 packages" in shelf
  link "Add a folder" in reader

# install PROJECT: from the empty Library, by keyboard. "Add a folder" opens
# the add-project dialog; the path is typed into its field and ↵ adds it.
part install PROJECT:path
do first-run
click "Add a folder" in reader
check add-dialog
  text "Choose folder…" "Cancel" "Add"
type "{PROJECT.abs}"
check path-typed-{PROJECT.name}
  text "{PROJECT.name} · a Rust project (Cargo.toml)"
key enter
do indexed {PROJECT}

# install-by-picker PROJECT: the same, choosing the folder in the native
# panel ("Choose folder…"). The panel is answered with PROJECT: the one
# substitution a journey may make (nothing can click an OS panel headless).
part install-by-picker PROJECT:path
do first-run
click "Add a folder" in reader
click "Choose folder…"
answer-picker {PROJECT}
do indexed {PROJECT}

# indexed PROJECT: the project is on the shelf and indexing; the owner's
# work is awaited in real time (its stages are logged as they appear: the
# project compiling, then each package its lock pins, "N of M"); then it is
# ready. A launch from Finder has none of the dev shell's toolchain
# variables: the app must find the person's own Rust, or the project stops.
part indexed PROJECT:path
check indexing-{PROJECT.name}
  text "{PROJECT.name}" in reader
  text "indexing" in reader
await absent "indexing" in reader within 40m
needs product "the app finds this Mac's Rust toolchain without the dev shell, so a Finder launch indexes the project (FINISH.md addendum: toolchain discovery)"
await absent "stopped" in reader within 5s
check installed-{PROJECT.name}
  text "{PROJECT.name}" in reader
  absent "indexing" "stopped" in reader
  saw "Compiling {PROJECT.name}." in reader

# install-all PROJECT: installed, and every package its lock pins is in the
# library too (the state journeys that browse the packages start from).
part install-all PROJECT:path
do install {PROJECT}
await like "All * packages {PROJECT.name} uses are in the library*" in reader within 40m
check installed-all-{PROJECT.name}
  like "All * packages {PROJECT.name} uses are in the library*" in reader
